This example shows how to use the KCW code to compute the band structure
of FCC Silicon using screening coefficients from a model dielectric 
function instead of the linear-response calculation (see example01).
Two model dielectric functions, both diagonal in G, are used: the
Gaussian model of PRM 2, 073803 (2018) and the model of Cappellini
et al., PRB 47, 9892 (1993) (see steps 4 and 6). The screening
coefficient of the Wannier function n is

   alpha_n = \sum_q <rho^n_q | eps^-1_q dV^n_q> / \sum_q <rho^n_q | dV^n_q>

with dV^n_q the Hxc potential generated by the Wannier density.
No linear-response problem is solved, so this step is much cheaper 
than the one in example01. The steps are:

1) run a standard scf calculation at DFT (LDA) level on a regular 2x2x2 mesh. 
   input=Si.scf.in, output=Si.scf.out

2) perform a separate wannierization for the occupied and empty manifold 
   results in ./wann_occ and ./wann_emp, respectively

3) prepare the KCW calculation 
   input=Si.kcw-wann2kcw.in, output=Si.kcw-wann2kcw.out

4) calculate the screening coefficients with the Gaussian model 
   (l_model_screen = .true., screen_model = 'gaussian'). The parameters 
   of the model are the macroscopic dielectric constant (eps_inf) and the 
   screening parameter mu (mu_screen, in bohr^-1). Here eps_inf = 12.24 
   (RPA at the PBE level) and mu = 0.66 bohr^-1 (PBE) for Si are taken 
   from Table IV of W. Chen et al., Phys. Rev. Mater. 2, 073803 (2018).
   Here l_vcut = .false., i.e. the q+G=0 term is neglected in both the
   numerator and the denominator. With l_vcut = .true. it is included
   (screened by eps_inf in the numerator) via the Gygi-Baldereschi scheme.
   input=Si.kcw-screen_gaussian.in, output=Si.kcw-screen_gaussian.out

5) calculate, interpolate and diagonalize the KI Hamiltonian with the 
   Gaussian-model screening coefficients
   input=Si.kcw-ham_gaussian.in, output=Si.kcw-ham_gaussian.out
   bands=Si.kcw_bands_gaussian.dat

6) calculate the screening coefficients with the Cappellini model
   dielectric function (screen_model = 'cappellini'):
      eps_q(G) = 1 + [ 1/(eps_inf-1) + alpha (|q+G|/q_TF)^2 + |q+G|^4/(4 w_p^2) ]^-1
   with alpha = 1.563 and q_TF, w_p from the average valence density
   (G. Cappellini et al., Phys. Rev. B 47, 9892 (1993)). Only eps_inf is
   needed.
   input=Si.kcw-screen_cappellini.in, output=Si.kcw-screen_cappellini.out

7) calculate, interpolate and diagonalize the KI Hamiltonian with the 
   Cappellini-model screening coefficients
   input=Si.kcw-ham_cappellini.in, output=Si.kcw-ham_cappellini.out
   bands=Si.kcw_bands_cappellini.dat

Each screening calculation overwrites the screening coefficients in the
temporary directory, so each KI Hamiltonian is run right after the 
corresponding screening calculation.

To compare with the ab-initio linear-response (LR) results of example01, 
run (after run_example, or directly on the files in reference/):

   ./compare_LR.sh

It prints the screening coefficients from the two models next to the LR
ones in ../example01/reference, with the relative difference, and the KI
minimum band gap and band gap at Gamma (from the interpolated band
structures along the k-path) with their difference wrt LR. It also writes
the gnuplot script compare_bands.gnu comparing the KI band structures
(each model vs LR, then all together). To see the plots:

   gnuplot compare_bands.gnu
